For a parametrization over $D$, use $$
A(S)=\iint_D\|\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v\|\,\mathrm dA
$$

Differentiate and form the fundamental vector product:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r_u&=\langle 1,0,0\rangle,\\\mathbf r_v&=\langle 0,2 v,2 v^{2}\rangle,\\\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf i&\mathbf j&\mathbf k\\1&0&0\\0&2 v&2 v^{2}\end{vmatrix}\\&=\langle 0,- 2 v^{2},2 v\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v\|&=\sqrt{4 v^{2} \left(v^{2} + 1\right)}\\&=2\sqrt{v^2}\sqrt{1+v^2}\\&=2 v \sqrt{v^{2} + 1}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $v\ge0$ throughout $D$, $\sqrt{v^2}=v$. Integrate first with respect to $u$, then use $w=1+v^2$, $\mathrm dw=2v\,\mathrm dv$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(S)&=\int_{1}^{\sqrt{3}}\int_{0}^{2}2 v \sqrt{v^{2} + 1}\,\mathrm du\,\mathrm dv\\&=2\int_{2}^{4}\sqrt w\,\mathrm dw\\&=2\left[\frac{2}{3}w^{3/2}\right]_{2}^{4}\\&=\frac{32}{3}-\left(\frac{8 \sqrt{2}}{3}\right)=\frac{32}{3} - \frac{8 \sqrt{2}}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

For a parametrization over $D$, use $$
A(S)=\iint_D\|\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v\|\,\mathrm dA
$$

Differentiate and form the fundamental vector product:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r_u&=\langle 2 u,v,0\rangle,\\\mathbf r_v&=\langle 0,u,v\rangle,\\\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf i&\mathbf j&\mathbf k\\2 u&v&0\\0&u&v\end{vmatrix}\\&=\langle v^{2},- 2 u v,2 u^{2}\rangle.
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v\|&=\sqrt{4 u^{4} + 4 u^{2} v^{2} + v^{4}}\\&=\sqrt{(v^2+2u^2)^2}\\&=v^2+2u^2.
\end{aligned}
$$

The last equality holds because $v^2+2u^2\ge0$. The parameter region is rectangular, so

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(S)&=\int_{2}^{4}\int_{0}^{1}(v^2+2u^2)\,\mathrm du\,\mathrm dv\\&=\int_{2}^{4}\left[uv^2+\frac{2}{3}u^3\right]_{0}^{1}\,\mathrm dv\\&=\int_{2}^{4}(v^{2} + \frac{2}{3})\,\mathrm dv\\&=\left[\frac{v \left(v^{2} + 2\right)}{3}\right]_{2}^{4}\\&=(24)-(4)=20.
\end{aligned}
$$

